WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:02.440
Film Courage: What thoughts do you have on
DIY release strategies?

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:06.050
Scott Kirkpatrick: Look…I guess the criticism
I get a lot (or the things I hear from people

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:09.050
all the time are) “I don’t understand
that businessey-stuff.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:10.750
I don’t get the distribution stuff.

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:16.430
I’m going to focus on making my movie and
someone else will deal with all that.”

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:25.710
The truth is today, it’s never been easier
to produce a movie or any type of media content

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.119
in general.

00:00:27.119 --> 00:00:33.030
The technology is fantastic, the costs have
dropped tremendously and anybody can do it.

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:36.399
The problem is that anyone can do it, there’s
tons of it.

00:00:36.399 --> 00:00:44.609
And so it’s more important now for filmmakers
and writers to be cognizant of what is needed

00:00:44.609 --> 00:00:48.519
in the marketplace and how that whole process
works.

00:00:48.519 --> 00:00:54.430
And the great thing is there has never been
a better time in this industry to do it all

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:56.140
on their own.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:59.360
Anybody can create a website, anybody can
create a blog, anybody can create a fanbase

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:01.360
using social media platforms.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:06.970
Anybody can spit out and leak information
about their title and build up an audience

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:08.550
and built up an interest.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:13.440
And when that magical moment, when that product
is ready to be released, already have a great

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.660
collection of people out there ready to buy
it.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:25.440
And do my view on it is there has never been
as much power in the hands of individuals

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:31.190
and individual content creators as there has
now and I think it’s only going to increase.

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:37.650
So I think the ability to take a project,
an idea, from beginning to end, cheaply, effectively

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:42.090
with very strong results has never been more
in the hands of individual filmmakers.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:48.360
So for those people who want to do their project
that is outside of the conventions of what

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.920
I talk about in Writing For The Green Light
[Scott’s book], they certainly can and they

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:53.240
should pursue what they want.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:57.170
But if you still product within the genre
conventions that I write about you’re going

00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:02.420
to see more traction on the professional side
because that’s going to stand out that they

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:07.730
were able to put a project like that together
and make a bit of profit on it for a very

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:08.730
cheap budget.

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:15.440
That’s someone who can prove to themselves,
we can bring them in and shoot films for us

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:17.600
or write films for us, whatever.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:21.820
Film Courage: What can filmmakers learn from
the app world?

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:26.360
Scott Kirkpatrick: Metrics.

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:34.240
The incredible amount of data and metrics
that go into engineering an app and how targeted

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:39.750
and focused it is for a very specific audience,
I think that’s what filmmakers can take

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:41.270
away from the app world.

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:47.150
What that opens the door to is the genre conventions
that I write about in Writing For The Green

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:52.520
Light are good on a grand scale, but some
people want to produce a documentary that’s

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:57.680
focused on a touchy issue that’s only important
to a very small minority of people, that’s

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:58.680
fine.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:03.000
We can exploit as much of that small percentage
of people as possible.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:08.700
If they focus it and target it and reach out
to those people.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:13.190
Same thing with faith-based content, there’s
a great audience there.

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:18.060
And the stuff that sometimes gets produced
on a grand scale isn’t hitting the nail

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:22.150
on the head the way that audience wants it,
they can do it themselves.

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:26.791
I think what we can learn from the app world
is how much data we have and how much of the

00:03:26.791 --> 00:03:29.640
answers we already have in our pockets or
purses.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:34.420
Film Courage: And just with the app world,
beta testing something, can a filmmaker really

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:35.610
do that with their movie?

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:40.230
Scott Kirkpatrick: It’s tough to beta test
a movie.

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:46.630
Beta testing I think it much better at platforms
and audience response and because of that

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:49.180
once you sort of make a movie, it’s sort
of one-and-done.

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:57.040
But it’s either going to resonate or it’s
not, so you can take as many steps as possible

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:59.880
for an audience to be receptive or not.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:05.580
But it’s the kind of thing where you’ve
kind of got to know the audience in advance

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:11.270
and that’s why the assembly line distribution
works the way it does where it’s all reverse-engineering,

00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:15.550
it starts with the audience and builds backs
to what should be produced versus producing

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:18.849
a movie and then hoping for the best that
it gets to the right audience.

